# kanso#1398 gave the PROVEN byte-index path a raw i64 phi beside its box, so a
# byte-discriminated dispatch could read one i64 instead of taking the box
# apart with an extractvalue pair, an icmp and a select. `emit_at` has a second
# path: when the inference cannot prove the container is bytes and the key an
# int, it tests both tags at runtime and falls back to `k_b_at_fast`. That
# merge got no raw phi, so the four instructions stayed.
#
# They stay on the SLOW arm here and leave the fast one. The in-range arm's
# byte is already an i64 and hands it straight to the phi; the arm that calls
# the runtime does the collapse beside the call, where it is one branch off the
# hot path rather than on it. 256 is the sentinel, and no byte can be 256.
#
# `source` answers bytes or `none`, so the container's set is BYTES|NONE: not
# provably bytes, which takes the general path, and with no LIST bit, which is
# what makes the merge's set safe to narrow -- a list slot holds whatever the
# list holds, thunks included, and `k_b_at`'s list case is the one arm that
# could answer one.
#
# Written against the printed answers rather than the emitted IR, because the
# IR is the current shape and the answers are the contract.
import "std/text"

fn source 1
  text/bytes "AB0"

fn source _
  none

fn kind none p
  "no source at {p}"

fn kind src p
  at_byte src[p] p

fn at_byte 65 _
  "A"

fn at_byte 66 _
  "B"

fn at_byte none p
  "none at {p}"

fn at_byte c p
  "byte {c} at {p}"

pub play = print "{kind (source 1) 1}"
  .> (_ -> print "{kind (source 1) 3}")
  .> (_ -> print "{kind (source 1) 4}")
  .> (_ -> print "{kind (source 1) 0}")
  .> (_ -> print "{kind (source 2) 1}")
